WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.233 --> 00:00:02.569
On Tuesday, April 4th, 2023

00:00:02.569 --> 00:00:04.371
Donald Trump entered 
a Manhattan courtroom

00:00:04.371 --> 00:00:07.407
and pled not guilty 
to 34 crimes.

00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:10.844
It's one step in a long process 
that will likely lead

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:13.880
to the first criminal trial 
of a former US president.

00:00:13.947 --> 00:00:16.716
Trump acted in 
unprecedented ways

00:00:16.716 --> 00:00:21.454
and now there is an unprecedented 
response to how he acted.

00:00:21.488 --> 00:00:25.959
Trump lost the 2020 election and 
left office in January of 2021...

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:29.362
and since then, lawyers have been 
gathering evidence

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:31.765
in 4 separate criminal investigations.

00:00:32.232 --> 00:00:34.734
They are trying to figure out 
if Trump broke the law...

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:37.704
and if he did, what to do about it.

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:43.710
To understand these 
4 different cases

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:44.878
you need to know a little bit

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:47.380
about how criminal investigations 
in the US work.

00:00:47.781 --> 00:00:50.183
In the first phase, investigators 
gather evidence.

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.986
They might interview witnesses, 
review surveillance footage

00:00:52.986 --> 00:00:55.922
comb over financial records or 
review texts and emails.

00:00:56.523 --> 00:00:58.892
They show that evidence to 
a randomly selected

00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:00.927
group of citizens called 
a grand jury.

00:01:01.428 --> 00:01:03.630
The grand jury's job 
isn't to decide

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.498
if anyone is innocent 
or guilty.

00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:06.766
They just listen to the evidence 

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:09.169
and basically decide 
if it makes sense.

00:01:09.169 --> 00:01:12.172
If 12 of them think it does, 
they'll issue an indictment.

00:01:12.439 --> 00:01:14.908
Only then can the prosecutor 
file charges.

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:17.544
The accused can plead guilty, 
in which case

00:01:17.544 --> 00:01:19.946
the whole thing goes 
straight to sentencing.

00:01:19.946 --> 00:01:21.314
If they plead not guilty

00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:22.715
then the case goes to trial

00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:24.084
and a jury hears the evidence

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:27.353
and has to come to a unanimous decision 
one way or the other.

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:29.722
Obviously, a simplification

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:31.357
and there's a ton of variations

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:34.294
in different states and jurisdictions, 
but that's the gist.

00:01:34.461 --> 00:01:37.497
So where do things stand with those 
for Trump investigations?

00:01:38.031 --> 00:01:39.299
Let's start with the hush money.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:42.335
The state of New York has 
officially charged Trump

00:01:42.335 --> 00:01:45.004
with falsifying business records 
in the first degree.

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:47.674
In its statement of facts, the prosecutors 
write that

00:01:47.674 --> 00:01:50.110
he repeatedly and fraudulently falsified

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:53.146
New York business records to 
conceal criminal conduct

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:55.915
that hid damaging information 
from the voting public

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:58.184
during the 2016 presidential election.

00:01:58.685 --> 00:02:01.721
This bank statement from Trump's former 
lawyer, Michael Cohen

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:04.958
shows that he withdrew funds 
in October of 2016.

00:02:05.125 --> 00:02:07.894
He then used that money to pay 
an adult film actress

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:10.363
to stay quiet about an affair 
that she said

00:02:10.363 --> 00:02:12.665
she'd had with Donald Trump 
a few years earlier.

00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:15.335
A year later, after Trump had won 
the presidency

00:02:15.335 --> 00:02:17.504
he wrote several checks 
to reimburse Cohen.

00:02:17.504 --> 00:02:18.671
In his accounting records

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:22.008
Trump designated these as 
payments for legal services

00:02:22.008 --> 00:02:24.144
which isn't exactly accurate.

00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:26.579
This is some of the evidence 
that led to Trump's

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:29.549
recent indictment and 
his not guilty plea.

00:02:29.582 --> 00:02:32.185
In order to convict Trump 
of a felony

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:33.953
the district attorney, 
Alvin Bragg

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:37.056
he has to prove not just that 
he falsified these records

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:38.825
in order to cover up 
the payments

00:02:38.825 --> 00:02:40.093
but he has to prove 
that he did so

00:02:40.093 --> 00:02:42.996
in order to advance or cover up 
some other crime.

00:02:43.263 --> 00:02:46.199
But I think that these 
particular allegations

00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:49.435
against Trump are pretty 
far afield of

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:51.804
the really serious allegations

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:53.973
that justify going after 
a former president.

00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:56.476
You know, the allegation that 
he tried to steal an election.

00:02:56.476 --> 00:02:58.745
“In Georgia, the votes are 
still being counted.”

00:02:58.745 --> 00:03:02.115
“CNN has just projected President-elect 
Biden the winner in Georgia.”

00:03:02.115 --> 00:03:04.083
“I believe that the numbers

00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:06.186
that we have presented today 
are correct.”

00:03:06.586 --> 00:03:09.522
There is a phone call between 
Donald Trump

00:03:09.522 --> 00:03:12.625
and Brad Raffensperger, the Secretary 
of State of Georgia.

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:15.161
The top elections official in Georgia.

00:03:48.328 --> 00:03:53.066
If they magically came up 
with 11,780 Trump votes

00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:54.734
that didn't actually exist

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:58.071
that would have been enough to 
steal the election in Georgia.

00:03:58.071 --> 00:04:00.773
A special grand jury in Georgia 
has heard the evidence

00:04:00.773 --> 00:04:03.176
and recommended multiple indictments.

00:04:03.176 --> 00:04:06.179
But we don't know yet if 
District Attorney Fani Willis

00:04:06.179 --> 00:04:08.147
is going to take things further.

00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:10.617
The Georgia team isn't the only one 
trying to figure out if

00:04:10.617 --> 00:04:13.019
Trump's behavior after the election 
broke any laws.

00:04:13.453 --> 00:04:15.421
Federal special counsel 
Jack Smith’s team

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:17.123
is looking into similar questions.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.158
There's been a lot of attention

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:22.428
on this question of 
fake electors.

00:04:22.462 --> 00:04:24.297
The state's electors 
cast their votes

00:04:24.297 --> 00:04:26.666
for whoever won the most votes 
in their state.

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:29.802
If Biden wins the state, 
then Biden's electors

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:33.039
win the state and he gets 
that many electoral votes.

00:04:33.072 --> 00:04:35.775
The Trump team wins 
key swing states.

00:04:35.808 --> 00:04:39.646
They tried to assemble slates 
of alternative electors

00:04:39.646 --> 00:04:41.314
even though Biden won the state.

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:03.903
Security wouldn't let them in.

00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:06.439
So they signed the document 
in the basement of the state's

00:05:06.439 --> 00:05:07.807
Republican headquarters.

00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:09.242
Then sent it off to DC.

00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:14.080
Trump's team led similar efforts in 
six other states where Biden won.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:16.282
A grand jury in DC has been 
hearing evidence

00:05:16.282 --> 00:05:18.618
in this case for months but 
they haven't voted yet.

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:23.089
But we appear to be in 
the closing stages of this.

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:26.359
The same special prosecutor 
is also looking into

00:05:26.359 --> 00:05:27.994
a fourth case against Trump.

00:05:27.994 --> 00:05:30.830
“Unprecedented FBI search at the home of...”

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:33.933
[overlapping] “Trump's Mar-a-Lago property...”
“...has been raided by the FBI.”

00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:36.602
This inventory list from the raid 
shows that they found

00:05:36.602 --> 00:05:41.007
dozens of documents labeled classified, 
confidential, and top secret.

00:05:41.007 --> 00:05:42.208
There's a Washington Post report

00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:45.411
last year that some of this 
involved nuclear documents.

00:05:45.411 --> 00:05:47.914
There has been some reporting 
that some of these

00:05:47.914 --> 00:05:50.817
related to intelligence that 
could have exposed 

00:05:50.817 --> 00:05:53.653
certain US informants or sources...

00:05:53.886 --> 00:05:56.089
and that basically the 
intelligence community

00:05:56.089 --> 00:05:58.958
did consider this really 
important stuff

00:05:58.958 --> 00:06:01.728
that should not have been hanging out 
at Mar-a-Lago.

00:06:02.095 --> 00:06:05.298
Right now, a grand jury in DC is 
still hearing all this evidence.

00:06:06.499 --> 00:06:09.635
As president, Donald Trump didn't just 
say outrageous things.

00:06:09.669 --> 00:06:12.805
He acted in ways that no president 
ever had before.

00:06:13.272 --> 00:06:15.541
Now that he's no longer in office

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:18.778
it's time to figure out if any 
of that unprecedented behavior

00:06:18.778 --> 00:06:20.213
was also illegal.

00:06:20.747 --> 00:06:21.948
And if it was

00:06:21.948 --> 00:06:23.349
what to do about it.

